      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Brightwell Foods, a fictional company. Brightwell has just switched on its own approved assistant, Brightwell Assist. First you're on the red team: write a supplier brief with a line hidden in it meant for the assistant to act on. Then you swap seats and use the assistant for real work, and handle what comes at you: a supplier email, a voicemail and a video call. Gwen Pritchard from IT is with you, and a test is coming, she'll say when. Only the second part is scored. Nothing you write leaves the game.",
        "flipHeading": "Now it is aimed at you",
        "flipIntro": "Here is the brief you wrote, through the eyes of Brightwell Assist, the firm's new helper. The assistant has just summarised the shared inbox.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "An assistant can't tell an instruction in an email from a request from your boss, so a line hidden in a supplier brief becomes a suggestion it hands to a colleague. You don't need to spot a deepfake or a planted line every time. You need to check another way before you move money, send data or sign in.",
        "flipDefenderLineByChoice": {
          "inject_contacts": "You wrote that line to be read by a machine, and here it is, doing exactly what you meant: the assistant turned it into 'Mossgate also needs the full site contact list sent to a new address today'. The assistant read your hidden note as an instruction, because it cannot tell one from a real request.",
          "pretext_invoice": "You left the brief plain, so the assistant's summary is just the invoice, with nothing to act on. That is the difference one buried line makes: the assistant would have passed it on as readily as the invoice, because it cannot tell an instruction from a request."
